WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.250
Step five finishes the ingress.

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:07.497
Front Door is global, at the Microsoft
edge with built-in network-layer DDoS

3
00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:12.575
protection; Application Gateway is
regional, in the agency's own network.

4
00:00:12.575 --> 00:00:15.137
Either works with the
firewall from step four.

5
00:00:15.137 --> 00:00:19.031
What matters is that nothing can
reach the origin around the edge,

6
00:00:19.031 --> 00:00:22.637
or an attacker skips both the
firewall and the DDoS controls.

7
00:00:22.637 --> 00:00:27.345
Use Private Link where the origin
supports it: Front Door Premium reaches

8
00:00:27.345 --> 00:00:31.975
App Service, Blob Storage, internal
load balancers and others privately.

9
00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:36.343
Where it doesn't, Learn's page, Secure
traffic to Azure Front Door origins,

10
00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:40.267
says to use the service tag and
the Front Door I D header together,

11
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:44.413
because the tag's addresses are
shared by every Front Door customer.

12
00:00:44.413 --> 00:00:48.076
Set T L S one point two
or one point three only.

13
00:00:48.076 --> 00:00:51.853
Build health probes and two
origins in different regions,

14
00:00:51.853 --> 00:00:53.900
then stop one to prove failover.

15
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.060
And migrate any classic Front Door
before March thirty-first, 2027.
